#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a6128-22df-5a02-96fd-7c8cabf8f5f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Complex Number in Polar Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b4055943-40a7-5920-9850-a9f1f50da9c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5089" :question/problem "Express $z=-5+12\\textrm{i}$ in the form $r(\\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)$. Round $\\theta$ to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $\\mid z \\mid$. For a complex number\n\n$$\nz=x+y\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nSo, for\n\n$$\nz = -5+12\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-5)^{2} + (12)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25 + 144} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{169} \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$ represents the argument $\\arg(z)$. Since $x = -5$ is negative and $y = 12$ is positive, $z$ lies in the second quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ = \\arg (z) &= π - \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\arctan \\mid \\frac{12}{-5} \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\arctan (\\frac{12}{5}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1.966\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we write the complex number\n\n$$\nz = -5 + 12\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nin polar form:\n\n$$\nz = 13(\\cos(1.966)+\\textrm{i}\\sin(1.966))\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31e4455a-9f8f-5c12-8c96-bb6735691591" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Polar Form to Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "e50fe1bc-2a1d-5913-b9d7-013c05baf246" :question/math-academy-id "e-5090" :question/problem "Express $z=4\\left(\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right)$ in the form $z=x+\\textrm{i}y$." :question/worked-solution "We will simplify the right-hand side. Since\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= 4(\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6})) \\\\\n&= 4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\textrm{i}\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&=-2\\sqrt{3} + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form"}] :question/id #uuid "3b103330-6aa7-5f1a-9a83-808515a43f97" :question/math-academy-id "e-5091" :question/problem "Express $z=\\dfrac{3}{2-\\textrm{i}}$ in the form $r(\\cos\\theta+\\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form\n\n$$\nz=x+y\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we remove $\\textrm{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $2+\\textrm{i}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\frac{3}{2 - \\textrm{i}} \\\\\n&= (3(2 + \\textrm{i}))/((2 - \\textrm{i})(2 + \\textrm{i})) \\\\\n&= (3(2 + \\textrm{i}))/(2^{2} + 1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}(2 + \\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{3}{5}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $\\mid z \\mid$. For a complex number\n\n$$\nz=x+y\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nSo, for\n\n$$\nz= \\dfrac{6}{5} +\\dfrac{3}{5}\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{6}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{3}{5})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{36 + 9}{25}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{45}{25}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta$ represents the argument $\\arg(z)$. Since\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nare both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg (z) &= \\arctan (((\\frac{3}{5}))/((\\frac{6}{5}))) \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= 0.464\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\left (\\cos(0.464)+ \\textrm{i} \\sin(0.464) \\right)\n$$"}]}
